    {
      "id": "JbQd-IenSwI89ZDRoOG0P",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1762.6546704293582,
      "y": 3904.0318140983973,
      "width": 893.6249884649536,
      "height": 3876.139199658692,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b01",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1589923310,
      "version": 3861,
      "versionNonce": 515361340,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "JDoE4v0xklM2Tphx1V8Y8",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1777184447787,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) How can we ensure that swiping is consistent and low latency?\n\n\n❌ The consistency challenge can be illustrated by:\n        → userA and userB swipe at roughly the same time\n        → Both users issue reads for the inverse swipe at the same time (but come up empty)\n        → Then both write their individual swipe relations\n\n→ This results in a Read Skew / Write Skew anomaly\n→ Isolation Means, concurrent transactions should behave as if they were executed serially.\n→ Cassandra offers serializable isolation, through Lightweight transactions on a partition key.\n→ But Cassandra LWTs are implemented using PAXOS (need multiple round trips across the\ncluster to reach concensus), which is more expensive than Isolation level/Row locking set in\nan RDBMS.\n→ RDBMS are a bad choice here because they don't support high write throughput (Usually\nsingle master)\n\n\n\n✅ GREAT SOLUTION: Sharded Cassandra with Single-Partition Transactions\n\n→ When we need consistency, we should immediately think transactions\n→ Can use cassandra's single-partition transactions to atomically handle swipes\n\n        1️⃣ First create a table with compound primary key that ensures swipes between same\nusers are in same partition\n\n        CREATE TABLE swipes (\n                user_pair text,      -- partition key: smaller_id:larger_id\n                from_user uuid,      -- clustering key\n                to_user uuid,        -- clustering key\n                direction text,\n                created_at timestamp,\n                PRIMARY KEY ((user_pair), from_user, to_user)\n            );\n\n        2️⃣ When a user swipes, we create the user_pair key by sorting the IDs to ensure\nconsistency\n\n        def get_user_pair(user_a, user_b):\n                # Sort IDs so (A->B) and (B->A) are in same partition\n                sorted_ids = sorted([user_a, user_b])\n                return f\"{sorted_ids[0]}:{sorted_ids[1]}\"\n        \n        def handle_swipe(from_user, to_user, direction):\n                user_pair = get_user_pair(from_user, to_user)\n                \n                # Both operations happen atomically in same partition\n                batch = \"\"\"\n                BEGIN BATCH\n                        INSERT INTO swipes (user_pair, from_user, to_user, direction, created_at)\n                        VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?);\n                        \n                        SELECT direction FROM swipes \n                        WHERE user_pair = ? \n                        AND from_user = to_user \n                        AND to_user = from_user;\n                APPLY BATCH;\n                \"\"\"\n\n→ By ensuring swipes between two users are stored in same partition, we can check for\nmatches without worrying about distributed transaction complexities.\n→ Partition key design eliminates need for cross-partition operations, making solution both\nperformant and reliable\n\n\nChallenges\n→ As swipes accumulate over time, partition sizes can grow significantly, potentially\nimpacting performance (mainly read, because we will need to go to oldest ss tables)\n→ Highly active users could create hot partitions that receive a disproportionate amount of\ntraffic\n→ Need a cleanup Job to archive old swipe data, preventing partitions from growing\nunbounded while preserving important historical data (deleted/banned users, maybe matched\nrecords?)\n\n\n\n✅ GREAT SOLUTION: Redis for Atomic Operations\n\n→ Redis is a better for for handling the consistency & latency requirements:\n        ⚫ Doesn't use PAXOS, it relies on single threaded nature and ability to execute lua\nscripts to provide feature similar to ACID transactions / shard level\n        ⚫ It is also faster for reads and writes(because of redis's in-memory nature), enabling\nlower latencies\n→ Cassandra excels at durability and storing large amounts of data, it's not designed for\natomic operations we need for real-time match detection\n→ We could use a hybrid approach here:\n        → Cassandra as our durable storage layer\n        → Redis to handle atomic swipe operations\n\n→ Still need to create key that combines both uids in a consistent way, so swipes between\nsame users land on the same shard\n\n                Key: \"swipes:123:456\"\n                Value: {\n                    \"123_swipe\": \"right\", // or yes/no\n                    \"456_swipe\": \"left\"   // or yes/no\n                }\n\n                def get_key(user_a, user_b):\n                        # Sort IDs so (A->B) and (B->A) map to same key\n                        sorted_ids = sorted([user_a, user_b])\n                        return f\"swipes:{sorted_ids[0]}:{sorted_ids[1]}\"\n                \n                def handle_swipe(from_user, to_user, direction):\n                        key = get_key(from_user, to_user)\n                        \n                        # Use Redis hash to store both users' swipes\n                        # Each hash has two fields: user1_swipe and user2_swipe\n                        script = \"\"\"\n                        redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], ARGV[1], ARGV[2])\n                        return redis.call('HGET', KEYS[1], ARGV[3])\n                        \"\"\"\n                        \n                        # Execute atomically using Lua script\n                        other_swipe = redis.eval(\n                            script,\n                            keys=[key],\n                            args=[\n                                f\"{from_user}_swipe\",  # field to set\n                                direction,             # our swipe\n                                f\"{to_user}_swipe\"     # field to check\n                            ]\n                        )\n                        \n                        # If other user swiped right too, it's a match!\n                        if direction == 'right' and other_swipe == 'right':\n                            create_match(from_user, to_user)\n\n→ Using Redis's atomic operations via Lua scripts, we can ensure swipe recording and match\nchecking happen as a single operation\n→ Redis hash slots will enable, scaling the cluster while keeping the key on the same\nshard/node\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Additional operational complexity instroduced for the redis cluster\n        ⚫ Handle node failures\n        ⚫ Handle rebalancing\nHowever these are more manageable than trying to achieve consistency in cassandra\n→ Memory management is another consideration, we can periodically flush swipe data to\nCassandra and maintain only recent swipes in Redis\n→ If we lose Redis data due to a node failure, we're only losing the ability to detect matches\nfor very recent swipes - users can always swipe again, & we're not losing historical record in\nCassandra\n→ Use a hybrid approach, system remains highly available and scalable while meeting our\ncore requirement of consistent, immediate match detection",
      "fontSize": 21.09463510018336,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) How can we ensure that swiping is consistent and low latency?\n\n\n❌ The consistency challenge can be illustrated by:\n        → userA and userB swipe at roughly the same time\n        → Both users issue reads for the inverse swipe at the same time (but come up empty)\n        → Then both write their individual swipe relations\n\n→ This results in a Read Skew / Write Skew anomaly\n→ Isolation Means, concurrent transactions should behave as if they were executed serially.\n→ Cassandra offers serializable isolation, through Lightweight transactions on a partition key.\n→ But Cassandra LWTs are implemented using PAXOS (need multiple round trips across the cluster to reach concensus), which is more expensive than Isolation level/Row locking set in an RDBMS.\n→ RDBMS are a bad choice here because they don't support high write throughput (Usually single master)\n\n\n\n✅ GREAT SOLUTION: Sharded Cassandra with Single-Partition Transactions\n\n→ When we need consistency, we should immediately think transactions\n→ Can use cassandra's single-partition transactions to atomically handle swipes\n\n        1️⃣ First create a table with compound primary key that ensures swipes between same users are in same partition\n\n        CREATE TABLE swipes (\n                user_pair text,      -- partition key: smaller_id:larger_id\n                from_user uuid,      -- clustering key\n                to_user uuid,        -- clustering key\n                direction text,\n                created_at timestamp,\n                PRIMARY KEY ((user_pair), from_user, to_user)\n            );\n\n        2️⃣ When a user swipes, we create the user_pair key by sorting the IDs to ensure consistency\n\n        def get_user_pair(user_a, user_b):\n                # Sort IDs so (A->B) and (B->A) are in same partition\n                sorted_ids = sorted([user_a, user_b])\n                return f\"{sorted_ids[0]}:{sorted_ids[1]}\"\n        \n        def handle_swipe(from_user, to_user, direction):\n                user_pair = get_user_pair(from_user, to_user)\n                \n                # Both operations happen atomically in same partition\n                batch = \"\"\"\n                BEGIN BATCH\n                        INSERT INTO swipes (user_pair, from_user, to_user, direction, created_at)\n                        VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?);\n                        \n                        SELECT direction FROM swipes \n                        WHERE user_pair = ? \n                        AND from_user = to_user \n                        AND to_user = from_user;\n                APPLY BATCH;\n                \"\"\"\n\n→ By ensuring swipes between two users are stored in same partition, we can check for matches without worrying about distributed transaction complexities.\n→ Partition key design eliminates need for cross-partition operations, making solution both performant and reliable\n\n\nChallenges\n→ As swipes accumulate over time, partition sizes can grow significantly, potentially impacting performance (mainly read, because we will need to go to oldest ss tables)\n→ Highly active users could create hot partitions that receive a disproportionate amount of traffic\n→ Need a cleanup Job to archive old swipe data, preventing partitions from growing unbounded while preserving important historical data (deleted/banned users, maybe matched records?)\n\n\n\n✅ GREAT SOLUTION: Redis for Atomic Operations\n\n→ Redis is a better for for handling the consistency & latency requirements:\n        ⚫ Doesn't use PAXOS, it relies on single threaded nature and ability to execute lua scripts to provide feature similar to ACID transactions / shard level\n        ⚫ It is also faster for reads and writes(because of redis's in-memory nature), enabling lower latencies\n→ Cassandra excels at durability and storing large amounts of data, it's not designed for atomic operations we need for real-time match detection\n→ We could use a hybrid approach here:\n        → Cassandra as our durable storage layer\n        → Redis to handle atomic swipe operations\n\n→ Still need to create key that combines both uids in a consistent way, so swipes between same users land on the same shard\n\n                Key: \"swipes:123:456\"\n                Value: {\n                    \"123_swipe\": \"right\", // or yes/no\n                    \"456_swipe\": \"left\"   // or yes/no\n                }\n\n                def get_key(user_a, user_b):\n                        # Sort IDs so (A->B) and (B->A) map to same key\n                        sorted_ids = sorted([user_a, user_b])\n                        return f\"swipes:{sorted_ids[0]}:{sorted_ids[1]}\"\n                \n                def handle_swipe(from_user, to_user, direction):\n                        key = get_key(from_user, to_user)\n                        \n                        # Use Redis hash to store both users' swipes\n                        # Each hash has two fields: user1_swipe and user2_swipe\n                        script = \"\"\"\n                        redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], ARGV[1], ARGV[2])\n                        return redis.call('HGET', KEYS[1], ARGV[3])\n                        \"\"\"\n                        \n                        # Execute atomically using Lua script\n                        other_swipe = redis.eval(\n                            script,\n                            keys=[key],\n                            args=[\n                                f\"{from_user}_swipe\",  # field to set\n                                direction,             # our swipe\n                                f\"{to_user}_swipe\"     # field to check\n                            ]\n                        )\n                        \n                        # If other user swiped right too, it's a match!\n                        if direction == 'right' and other_swipe == 'right':\n                            create_match(from_user, to_user)\n\n→ Using Redis's atomic operations via Lua scripts, we can ensure swipe recording and match checking happen as a single operation\n→ Redis hash slots will enable, scaling the cluster while keeping the key on the same shard/node\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Additional operational complexity instroduced for the redis cluster\n        ⚫ Handle node failures\n        ⚫ Handle rebalancing\nHowever these are more manageable than trying to achieve consistency in cassandra\n→ Memory management is another consideration, we can periodically flush swipe data to Cassandra and maintain only recent swipes in Redis\n→ If we lose Redis data due to a node failure, we're only losing the ability to detect matches for very recent swipes - users can always swipe again, & we're not losing historical record in Cassandra\n→ Use a hybrid approach, system remains highly available and scalable while meeting our core requirement of consistent, immediate match detection",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pIPigoQ79kMTSulNiV_td",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3782.255113014491,
      "y": 3868.4287740570844,
      "width": 898.2567432211613,
      "height": 1875,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b06",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1412218762,
      "version": 6234,
      "versionNonce": 1942355762,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1769093778873,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2) How can we ensure low latency for feed/stack generation?\n\n\n❌ Current design has us running a slow query every time we want a new stack of users\n\nSELECT * FROM users\n        WHERE age BETWEEN 18 AND 35\nAND interestedIn = 'female'\nAND lat BETWEEN userLat - maxDistance AND userLat + maxDistance\nAND long BETWEEN userLong - maxDistance AND userLong + maxDistance\n\n\n\n⚠️ GOOD SOLUTION: Use of Indexed Databases for Real-Time Querying\n\n→ Creating indexes on fields most commonly used in feed generation, (user interests, age range, &\ngeospatial location), PostgreSQL supports most of these indexes\n        ⚫ RDBMS would not work too well at this scale, \n        ⚫ They are not optimized for search\n        ⚫ Limits on number of read replicas\n        ⚫ Master & read replicas can only be veritically scaled \n        ⚫ Many joins, cannot cache intermediate results\n        ⚫ No support for results ranking and scoring\n\n→ Better still, a search-optimized database such as Elasticsearch can be used, as they are designed\nto scale & handle the search performance requirements of a system like tinder better\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Maintaining data consistency between the RDBMS & indexed search database\n→ Can be solved using CDC. Could use a batching strategy to reduce number of writes to the\nindexed database, since Elasticsearch is optimized for read-heavy & not write-heavy workloads\n\n\n\n⚠️ GOOD SOLUTION: Pre-computation and Caching\n\n→ Another way would be to precompute and cache user feeds asynchronously\n→ Periodic background jobs can generate feeds based on users’ preferences and location & store\nthem in a cache for instant retrieval (No need for real-time computation)\n→ Pre-computation can be scheduled during off-peak hours to reduce impact on system resources\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Primary challenge is that, highly active users may quickly exhaust their cached feeds, leading to\ndelays while new matches are generated or fetched (using expensive queries)\n→ Also, pre-computed feeds not have most recent changes in user profiles, preferences, or new\nusers. Resulting in less relevant matches and bad user experience\n⛔ This alone would most prob not work\n\n\n\n✅ GREAT SOLUTION: Combination of Pre-computation and Indexed Database\n\n→ Periodically pre-compute and cache feeds for users based on their preferences & location,\nallowing for low latency feed fetching containing accurate data\n→ As we record matched data for each swipe, we are aware of which users to be populated into\nthe feed to avoid duplicates \n→ As users exhaust their cached feed, system seamlessly transitions to generating additional\nmatches in real-time, by leveraging Elasticsearch indexes\n→ We can also trigger generation of a new feed, when a user has a few profiles left to swipe\nthrough\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Stale feeds\n        → User changed locations\n        → User changed preferences (interests, age range, etc)\n→ Changes can trigger a feed refresh in the background, through DB triggers / CDC\n        → Cache could also be expired, but it is preferrable to populate feed here\n→ Good to build tunable parameters into the solution. Few could be:\n        → TTL for cached profiles (< 1hr)\n        → Number of profiles cached\n        → Set of users we are caching feeds for (warm cache only active users)\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2) How can we ensure low latency for feed/stack generation?\n\n\n❌ Current design has us running a slow query every time we want a new stack of users\n\nSELECT * FROM users\n        WHERE age BETWEEN 18 AND 35\nAND interestedIn = 'female'\nAND lat BETWEEN userLat - maxDistance AND userLat + maxDistance\nAND long BETWEEN userLong - maxDistance AND userLong + maxDistance\n\n\n\n⚠️ GOOD SOLUTION: Use of Indexed Databases for Real-Time Querying\n\n→ Creating indexes on fields most commonly used in feed generation, (user interests, age range, & geospatial location), PostgreSQL supports most of these indexes\n        ⚫ RDBMS would not work too well at this scale, \n        ⚫ They are not optimized for search\n        ⚫ Limits on number of read replicas\n        ⚫ Master & read replicas can only be veritically scaled \n        ⚫ Many joins, cannot cache intermediate results\n        ⚫ No support for results ranking and scoring\n\n→ Better still, a search-optimized database such as Elasticsearch can be used, as they are designed to scale & handle the search performance requirements of a system like tinder better\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Maintaining data consistency between the RDBMS & indexed search database\n→ Can be solved using CDC. Could use a batching strategy to reduce number of writes to the indexed database, since Elasticsearch is optimized for read-heavy & not write-heavy workloads\n\n\n\n⚠️ GOOD SOLUTION: Pre-computation and Caching\n\n→ Another way would be to precompute and cache user feeds asynchronously\n→ Periodic background jobs can generate feeds based on users’ preferences and location & store them in a cache for instant retrieval (No need for real-time computation)\n→ Pre-computation can be scheduled during off-peak hours to reduce impact on system resources\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Primary challenge is that, highly active users may quickly exhaust their cached feeds, leading to delays while new matches are generated or fetched (using expensive queries)\n→ Also, pre-computed feeds not have most recent changes in user profiles, preferences, or new users. Resulting in less relevant matches and bad user experience\n⛔ This alone would most prob not work\n\n\n\n✅ GREAT SOLUTION: Combination of Pre-computation and Indexed Database\n\n→ Periodically pre-compute and cache feeds for users based on their preferences & location, allowing for low latency feed fetching containing accurate data\n→ As we record matched data for each swipe, we are aware of which users to be populated into the feed to avoid duplicates \n→ As users exhaust their cached feed, system seamlessly transitions to generating additional matches in real-time, by leveraging Elasticsearch indexes\n→ We can also trigger generation of a new feed, when a user has a few profiles left to swipe through\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Stale feeds\n        → User changed locations\n        → User changed preferences (interests, age range, etc)\n→ Changes can trigger a feed refresh in the background, through DB triggers / CDC\n        → Cache could also be expired, but it is preferrable to populate feed here\n→ Good to build tunable parameters into the solution. Few could be:\n        → TTL for cached profiles (< 1hr)\n        → Number of profiles cached\n        → Set of users we are caching feeds for (warm cache only active users)\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "3YCUGV7y8MJ4GcRq52F-n",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4874.995629372402,
      "y": 3856.8422716580853,
      "width": 797.7952025196778,
      "height": 900,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b07",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1000709334,
      "version": 4288,
      "versionNonce": 1874319424,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1769104635082,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "3) How can the system avoid showing user profiles that the user has previously swiped\non?\n\n\nBAD SOLUTION: DB Query + Contains Check\n\n→ Profile service queries swipe database & does contains check to filter out users who\nhave been swiped on before\n→ Query to get swiped-on profiles will be efficient because it is routed to correct\npartition based on swiping_user_id\n\nChallenges\n→ Because A>>C, Not all swipe data might have \"landed\" in all replicas of a partition\nwhen feed is being generated. We risk missing swipes, & re-showing profiles\n→ If user has large swipe history, there might be a lot of user IDs returned, & contains\ncheck will get progressively more expensive\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Cache + DB Query + Contains Check\n\n→ We can consider doing contains queries on the backend & adding a cache for recent\nuser swipes to avoid the problems presented by availability-skewed cassandra\n→ Managing a backend cache just to store data before it is written to each cassandra\npartition would be expensive, because although storage costs would be < than if it was\nper user, reads/writes would be as high as for all swipes (sum of all users)\n→ Instead we can use the client to store recent swipe data (K most recent swipes) &\nhave the client filter out profiles from feed responses\n→ Close to depleting their stack of profiles while swiping, the client can pre-fetch the\nnext set of recommended profiles, the client cache size can be increased here to\naccomodate the remainder due to pre-fetch\n\n\nChallenges\n→ This solution is still subjected to the problems created by users with extensive swipe\nhistories and large user ID contains checks that get slower as the user swipes more.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "3) How can the system avoid showing user profiles that the user has previously swiped on?\n\n\nBAD SOLUTION: DB Query + Contains Check\n\n→ Profile service queries swipe database & does contains check to filter out users who have been swiped on before\n→ Query to get swiped-on profiles will be efficient because it is routed to correct partition based on swiping_user_id\n\nChallenges\n→ Because A>>C, Not all swipe data might have \"landed\" in all replicas of a partition when feed is being generated. We risk missing swipes, & re-showing profiles\n→ If user has large swipe history, there might be a lot of user IDs returned, & contains check will get progressively more expensive\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Cache + DB Query + Contains Check\n\n→ We can consider doing contains queries on the backend & adding a cache for recent user swipes to avoid the problems presented by availability-skewed cassandra\n→ Managing a backend cache just to store data before it is written to each cassandra partition would be expensive, because although storage costs would be < than if it was per user, reads/writes would be as high as for all swipes (sum of all users)\n→ Instead we can use the client to store recent swipe data (K most recent swipes) & have the client filter out profiles from feed responses\n→ Close to depleting their stack of profiles while swiping, the client can pre-fetch the next set of recommended profiles, the client cache size can be increased here to accomodate the remainder due to pre-fetch\n\n\nChallenges\n→ This solution is still subjected to the problems created by users with extensive swipe histories and large user ID contains checks that get slower as the user swipes more.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
