        }
      ],
      "updated": 1769104874255,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "dyZsdEXKJ_7no4mukpZMV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2828.8861000160964,
      "y": 4916.883883892447,
      "width": 731.0792236328125,
      "height": 1275,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2D8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1302423045,
      "version": 987,
      "versionNonce": 882672576,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1769104874255,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why this makes the solution performant\n\n    Cross-partition operations cause:\n            → Multiple network hops\n            → Coordinator fan-out\n            → Increased tail latency\n\n    Single-partition operations:\n            → Go to one replica set\n            → Touch fewer nodes\n            → Scale linearly\n\n    So latency stays low even under load.\n\nWhy this makes the solution more reliable\n\n    Reliability here means:\n            → Fewer failure modes\n            → No partial updates across nodes\n            → No distributed locks or sagas\n\n    You’ve reduced:\n            → Race windows\n            → Inconsistent reads\n            → Coordination complexity\n\n    But you have not eliminated all failure.\n\nImportant caveats (this is where senior answers shine)\n\n    ⚠️ 1. LWTs are still expensive\n        Even in one partition:\n                → Paxos is slow\n                → Throughput is limited\n\n    So this approach is theoretically correct, but operationally risky at Tinder scale.\n\n    ⚠️ 2. Other system flows still cross partitions\n        Examples:\n                → Fetching a feed of candidates\n                → Analytics queries\n                → Recommendation pipelines\n                → Reconciliation jobs\n\n    Those absolutely involve cross-partition reads.\n\n    ⚠️ 3. Reliability ≠ strong consistency everywhere\n    You only get strong guarantees:\n            → For that partition\n            → For that operation\n            → On that code path",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "8hef3_EBfMLtKrVHaCbkm",
      "originalText": "Why this makes the solution performant\n\n    Cross-partition operations cause:\n            → Multiple network hops\n            → Coordinator fan-out\n            → Increased tail latency\n\n    Single-partition operations:\n            → Go to one replica set\n            → Touch fewer nodes\n            → Scale linearly\n\n    So latency stays low even under load.\n\nWhy this makes the solution more reliable\n\n    Reliability here means:\n            → Fewer failure modes\n            → No partial updates across nodes\n            → No distributed locks or sagas\n\n    You’ve reduced:\n            → Race windows\n            → Inconsistent reads\n            → Coordination complexity\n\n    But you have not eliminated all failure.\n\nImportant caveats (this is where senior answers shine)\n\n    ⚠️ 1. LWTs are still expensive\n        Even in one partition:\n                → Paxos is slow\n                → Throughput is limited\n\n    So this approach is theoretically correct, but operationally risky at Tinder scale.\n\n    ⚠️ 2. Other system flows still cross partitions\n        Examples:\n                → Fetching a feed of candidates\n                → Analytics queries\n                → Recommendation pipelines\n                → Reconciliation jobs\n\n    Those absolutely involve cross-partition reads.\n\n    ⚠️ 3. Reliability ≠ strong consistency everywhere\n    You only get strong guarantees:\n            → For that partition\n            → For that operation\n            → On that code path",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "UAlDPhEtH2SC5KBUoK_um",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": -307.4403416355607,
      "y": 3787.33941461735,
      "width": 897.9294415624289,
      "height": 4484.987140965786,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
