      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "qDrih9GOIxJXI3SjmEH9H",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 731.5924783351502,
      "y": 3815.8049373832228,
      "width": 887.7590942382812,
      "height": 3600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2O",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1487216434,
      "version": 787,
      "versionNonce": 1807132846,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1769017502395,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Can there be a scenario where we write half a swipe to cassandra, that is no longer present in\nredis. What happens when we get the other half?\n\nYes — that scenario absolutely can happen, and good systems explicitly design for it. This is one\nof the most important failure modes in the Redis + Cassandra hybrid design, and interviewers love\nwhen candidates call it out.\n\nLet’s walk through it carefully.\n\n1️⃣ The exact failure scenario\n\nAssume this flow:\n        1. User A swipes right on B\n        2. We write to Redis (atomic, fast)\n        3. We asynchronously write to Cassandra\n        4. Redis key expires or the node crashes\n        5. Cassandra write succeeded\n\nNow the system state is:\n\n                    Store                              State\n                    Redis                              ❌ No swipe record\n                    Cassandra                      ✅ A → B = right\n\nLater:\n        6. User B swipes right on A\n\nRedis sees:\n        → No existing swipe from A\n        → So no match is detected immediately\n\nThis is exactly the “half swipe” problem you’re describing.\n\n2️⃣ Why this is acceptable (and intentional)\n\nThis architecture chooses correctness at the moment of detection, not perfect synchronization\nbetween stores.\n\nRedis is the source of truth for real-time matching. Cassandra is the source of truth for history.\nIf Redis loses state:\n        → We might miss an instant match\n        → But we do not lose the swipe\nThis is an availability-first decision.\n\n3️⃣ What happens when the other half arrives?\n    Case: B swipes after Redis lost A’s swipe\n        1. Redis check → no reciprocal swipe\n        2. No immediate match notification\n        3. B’s swipe is written to:\n                → Redis\n                → Cassandra\n\n    Now Cassandra contains:\n        A → B = right\n        B → A = right\n        \n    We have a latent match.\n\n4️⃣ How the system recovers (this is the key part)\n\nA) Background reconciliation job (most common)\n\n    A periodic job scans Cassandra for:\n            → Reciprocal swipes\n            → No corresponding match record\n\n    When found:\n            → Create the match\n            → Send notifications\n\n    From the user’s perspective:\n            → “Oh, they just swiped right!”\n            → Totally believable UX\n\n    This is exactly what the original write-up hinted at.\n\nB) Read repair on swipe (stronger variant)\n\n    When B swipes:\n            → Check Redis first\n            → If no match:\n                    Optionally query Cassandra\n                    Look for A → B\n\n    This adds:\n            → Slight latency\n            → Rare Cassandra read\n\n    But only happens on Redis miss, which should be rare.\n    This is a common fast-path / slow-path design.\n\nC) Match creation is idempotent\n\n    Match creation must be idempotent\n    Even if:\n            → Redis detects match\n            → AND reconciliation detects it later\n\n    Only one match record is created.\n    Example:\n        INSERT INTO matches (user1, user2)\n        IF NOT EXISTS\n\n    This avoids duplicates.\n\n5️⃣ Why we don’t try to “fix” this with transactions\n\n    Trying to make Redis + Cassandra fully transactional would:\n            → Kill latency\n            → Introduce distributed coordination\n            → Reduce availability\n            → Increase operational risk\n\n    At Tinder scale: Eventual match detection is far better than slow or unreliable swiping.\n\n6️⃣ How often does this actually happen?\n\n    In practice:\n            → Redis TTLs are tuned\n            → Write-through caches reduce loss\n            → Replicated Redis clusters minimize crashes\n\n    So:\n            → The “half swipe” window is small\n            → Reconciliation volume is tiny relative to total swipes\n\n7️⃣ Why this is still a good design\n\n\n                Property                                                      Achieved\n                Low latency                                                ✅ Redis\n                Strong real-time consistency                   ✅ Redis\n                Durability                                                    ✅ Cassandra\n                High availability                                         ✅ Both\n                Graceful degradation                                ✅ Reconciliation\n\n8️⃣ One-line interview answer\n\n“Yes, we can lose a swipe in Redis while it’s already persisted in Cassandra. When the reciprocal\nswipe arrives, we won’t detect the match immediately, but reconciliation or a Cassandra slow-path\nwill create the match later. This is an intentional trade-off to keep swipe latency low and the\nsystem highly available.”\n\nIf you say that, you’re operating at staff+ level.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "KctqE0VHd1bM1_wNIAxb1",
      "originalText": "Can there be a scenario where we write half a swipe to cassandra, that is no longer present in redis. What happens when we get the other half?\n\nYes — that scenario absolutely can happen, and good systems explicitly design for it. This is one of the most important failure modes in the Redis + Cassandra hybrid design, and interviewers love when candidates call it out.\n\nLet’s walk through it carefully.\n\n1️⃣ The exact failure scenario\n\nAssume this flow:\n        1. User A swipes right on B\n        2. We write to Redis (atomic, fast)\n        3. We asynchronously write to Cassandra\n        4. Redis key expires or the node crashes\n        5. Cassandra write succeeded\n\nNow the system state is:\n\n                    Store                              State\n                    Redis                              ❌ No swipe record\n                    Cassandra                      ✅ A → B = right\n\nLater:\n        6. User B swipes right on A\n\nRedis sees:\n        → No existing swipe from A\n        → So no match is detected immediately\n\nThis is exactly the “half swipe” problem you’re describing.\n\n2️⃣ Why this is acceptable (and intentional)\n\nThis architecture chooses correctness at the moment of detection, not perfect synchronization between stores.\n\nRedis is the source of truth for real-time matching. Cassandra is the source of truth for history.\nIf Redis loses state:\n        → We might miss an instant match\n        → But we do not lose the swipe\nThis is an availability-first decision.\n\n3️⃣ What happens when the other half arrives?\n    Case: B swipes after Redis lost A’s swipe\n        1. Redis check → no reciprocal swipe\n        2. No immediate match notification\n        3. B’s swipe is written to:\n                → Redis\n                → Cassandra\n\n    Now Cassandra contains:\n        A → B = right\n        B → A = right\n        \n    We have a latent match.\n\n4️⃣ How the system recovers (this is the key part)\n\nA) Background reconciliation job (most common)\n\n    A periodic job scans Cassandra for:\n            → Reciprocal swipes\n            → No corresponding match record\n\n    When found:\n            → Create the match\n            → Send notifications\n\n    From the user’s perspective:\n            → “Oh, they just swiped right!”\n            → Totally believable UX\n\n    This is exactly what the original write-up hinted at.\n\nB) Read repair on swipe (stronger variant)\n\n    When B swipes:\n            → Check Redis first\n            → If no match:\n                    Optionally query Cassandra\n                    Look for A → B\n\n    This adds:\n            → Slight latency\n            → Rare Cassandra read\n\n    But only happens on Redis miss, which should be rare.\n    This is a common fast-path / slow-path design.\n\nC) Match creation is idempotent\n\n    Match creation must be idempotent\n    Even if:\n            → Redis detects match\n            → AND reconciliation detects it later\n\n    Only one match record is created.\n    Example:\n        INSERT INTO matches (user1, user2)\n        IF NOT EXISTS\n\n    This avoids duplicates.\n\n5️⃣ Why we don’t try to “fix” this with transactions\n\n    Trying to make Redis + Cassandra fully transactional would:\n            → Kill latency\n            → Introduce distributed coordination\n            → Reduce availability\n            → Increase operational risk\n\n    At Tinder scale: Eventual match detection is far better than slow or unreliable swiping.\n\n6️⃣ How often does this actually happen?\n\n    In practice:\n            → Redis TTLs are tuned\n            → Write-through caches reduce loss\n            → Replicated Redis clusters minimize crashes\n\n    So:\n            → The “half swipe” window is small\n            → Reconciliation volume is tiny relative to total swipes\n\n7️⃣ Why this is still a good design\n\n\n                Property                                                      Achieved\n                Low latency                                                ✅ Redis\n                Strong real-time consistency                   ✅ Redis\n                Durability                                                    ✅ Cassandra\n                High availability                                         ✅ Both\n                Graceful degradation                                ✅ Reconciliation\n\n8️⃣ One-line interview answer\n\n“Yes, we can lose a swipe in Redis while it’s already persisted in Cassandra. When the reciprocal swipe arrives, we won’t detect the match immediately, but reconciliation or a Cassandra slow-path will create the match later. This is an intentional trade-off to keep swipe latency low and the system highly available.”\n\nIf you say that, you’re operating at staff+ level.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
